(FIL-06: accessed is a Unix timestamp Number.
 Also covers: FIL-05 (modified, the same shape).
 Both bounded on both sides: not less than 1 (never 0/negative), and
 never after `current time's unix` - the manual DOES give a clock
 (LANGUAGE.md 4503-4552, "Getting Current Time" / "Inline Time Access"),
 so "not in the future relative to now" is assertable exactly, not just
 as a wide fixed floor. `current time's unix` used inline inside an If
 condition, not stored first - verified to work the same as the
 documented inline Print form.
 On SUCCESS this prints a fixed marker, "FIL-0N in range", never the raw
 timestamp: a first version of this leaf printed the observed value the
 way every other assertion helper here does, and a live campaign (200
 programs, seed 5001) caught it as a `nondeterministic` finding within
 20 programs - not a compiler bug, but this program's own file's mtime
 legitimately ticking a wall-clock second between the oracle's two
 back-to-back runs of the identical binary. The bound-vs-subject checks
 are exactly as strict either way; only what prints on success changed
 (the same tradeoff 'gen leaf timer and clock' already makes, src/
 gen_misc.vox, for the same reason).
 Run from the vox-fuzz worktree root; the probe creates everything it
 needs under vf_scratch/files/ and touches nothing else.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/usr/share/vox/coreasm /usr/bin/vox docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-06.vox -o p && ./p (twice, back to back)
 actual output (both runs, identical - the point of the fix):
   FIL-05 in range
   FIL-06 in range
 Then with the accessed lower-bound line changed to `is less than
 9999999999` (a deliberately wrong expectation, since a real epoch
 timestamp today is far below ten billion), to prove a failing
 assertion is classified correctly:
   FIL-05 in range
   ASSERT FIL-06: expected at least 9999999999 got 1787989903
 exit code 95.
)
Create a directory called "vf_scratch".
Create a directory called "vf_scratch/files".
open a file for writing called 'the drawer' at "vf_scratch/files/fil06.txt".
Write "seed" to 'the drawer'.
If 'the drawer's modified is less than 1 then, Print "ASSERT FIL-05: expected at least 1 got {'the drawer's modified}", Exit 95. If 'the drawer's modified is greater than current time's unix then, Print "ASSERT FIL-05: expected at most {current time's unix} got {'the drawer's modified}", Exit 95. Print "FIL-05 in range".
If 'the drawer's accessed is less than 1 then, Print "ASSERT FIL-06: expected at least 1 got {'the drawer's accessed}", Exit 95. If 'the drawer's accessed is greater than current time's unix then, Print "ASSERT FIL-06: expected at most {current time's unix} got {'the drawer's accessed}", Exit 95. Print "FIL-06 in range".
Close 'the drawer'.
